The process for injecting  adversarial attacks into the classical Reinforcement Learning (RL) loop is shown in Figure \ref{fig:attack_loop}. Importantly, instead of directly altering the underlying state variable $s_t$ that influences the next step of the environment dynamics, our adversary is only allowed to change \textit{the agent's perception of} $s_t$ by sending a perturbed adversarial observation $o_t$ to the agent. As such, the adversary can only influence environment dynamics indirectly via the agent -- the attacks engineer $o_t$ in an attempt to control or alter the agent's action $a_t$.

