The results show that most channel samples are simple inputs, which do not require reaching the maximum iterations $\tau$ as set in the simulation.}
As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig: ICEvsCE}, ICENet has an average of 2.215 iterations (average depth due to varying input needs). Additionally, to achieve better performance, we cascade 4 lightweight blocks in one ICENet-IEB. Therefore, the ICENet is compared with an ECENet stacking with $\lceil2.215\times4\rceil \approx 9$ lightweight blocks. It can be seen that ICENet achieves better performance with fewer layers, compared with the 9-block ECENet, displaying its ability to dynamically adjust the network structure according to the complexity of the samples.
For some channel samples, excessive iterations may lead to performance degradation, while ICENet adaptively reduces unnecessary iterations, thus ensuring superior overall performance.

