% $$\type_T^S(v)\coloneqq \{s \in S ~|~ (s, v) \in A(T) \}$$  

% We can partition all non-$S$ vertices into equivalence classes, where each equivalence class contains vertices of the same type. More formally, let $\eqv_T^S[X]$ be the set of all non-$S$ vertices $v$ for which $\type_T^S(v) = X$, that is, $ \eqv_T^S[X] \coloneq \{v~|~\type_T^S(v) = X\}$. We say $\eqv_T^S[X]$ is \textit{non-trivial} when $\eqv_T^S[X] \neq \emptyset$. Note that there are at most $2^{|S|}$ many  equivalence classes.

Observe that each non-\( S \) vertex \( v \in V(T) \setminus S \) divides the \( S \) vertices into two groups: one group contains all of its in-neighbors from \( S \), and the other contains all of its out-neighbors from \( S \) in \( T \). To encapsulate this phenomenon, we define a function \( \type_T^S \colon V(T) \setminus S \to 2^S \) as follows: for every vertex \( v \in V(T) \setminus S \),
$$
\type_T^S(v) \coloneqq \{ s \in S \mid (s, v) \in A(T) \}.
