\begin{abstract}
% With remarkable capabilities, large Language Models (LLMs) have revolutionized natural language processing (NLP), sparking a new demand to deploy LLMs from cloud to edge. However, GPUs are hindered by high power consumption. FPGAs present a compelling alternative with their reconfigurable architecture and superior energy efficiency. Nonetheless, deploying LLMs on FPGAs is challenging due to their substantial computation and memory requirements. While prior works have explored quantization and pruning for LLM, these methods present limited compression ratio for maintaining model accuracy. Additionally, maximizing the potential of pretrained LLMs on FPGAs requires addressing challenges such as generalization beyond pretrained sequence lengths (e.g., the 4K token limit of Llama-2) and the growing memory demands of the key-value (KV) cache. To overcome these challenges, we propose LiteLLM, a framework for efficient LLM inference on FPGAs through algorithm and hardware co-design. First, we introduce LiteCompress, a powerful compression engine that achieve W$2$A$8$KV$4$ combined with $2$:$4$ sparsity and sliding attention window to significantly reduce computation and memory requirements while enabling generalization to longer sequence lengths. Then, we design a specialized FPGA accelerator with a reconfigurable computing engine optimized for processing the diverse computational requirements (diverse precisions, sparse/dense operation, and matrix-matrix/matrix-vector multiplication) in compressed LLMs. Finally, we validate LiteLLM on the Xilinx Alveo U280 FPGA, demonstrating a \lyb{$\uparrow$$\mathbf{xx}\%$} improvement in energy efficiency and a \lyb{$\uparrow$$\mathbf{xx}\%$} increase in throughput compared to the state-of-the-art FlightLLM.
Recently, large language models (LLMs) have achieved huge success in the natural language processing (NLP) field, driving a growing demand to extend their deployment from the cloud to edge devices. However, deploying LLMs on resource-constrained edge devices poses significant challenges, including (1) intensive computations and huge model sizes, (2) great memory and bandwidth demands introduced by the autoregressive generation process, and (3) limited scalability for handling long sequences.
To address these challenges, we propose AccLLM, a comprehensive acceleration framework that enables efficient and fast long-context LLM inference through algorithm and hardware co-design. At the algorithmic level, we integrate (1) pruning, (2) $\Lambda$-shaped attention, and (3) an innovative W$2$A$8$KV$4$ ($2$-bit weights, $8$-bit activations, and $4$-bit KV cache) quantization scheme, thus effectively reducing memory and bandwidth requirements while facilitating LLMs' long-sequence generation.
At the hardware level, we design a dedicated FPGA-based accelerator with a reconfigurable computing engine to effectively and flexibly accommodate diverse operations arising from our compression algorithm, thereby fully translating the algorithmic innovations into tangible hardware efficiency.
We validate AccLLM on the Xilinx Alveo U280 FPGA, demonstrating a \lyb{$\uparrow$$\mathbf{4.07}\times$} energy efficiency and a \lyb{$\uparrow$$\mathbf{2.98}\times$} throughput compared to the state-of-the-art work FlightLLM.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Large language models, quantization, pruning, compression, acceleration, algorithm-hardware co-design.

