\vspace{0.5ex}\noindent \textbf{Discussions on How to Retrieve and Refine Candidate Subgraphs:} Algorithm~\ref{alg:css} illustrates the pseudo code of a recursive function to retrieve and refine candidate subgraphs. Specifically, in the base case that the recursive depth $dep$ is $|Q|$, we have all the matching pairs of vertices between $M$ and $Q$, and check the S$^3$AND constraints between subgraph $g$ (with vertices in $M$) and query graph $q$. If candidate subgraph $g$ is the S$^3$AND query answer, then we add $g$ to the answer set $S$ (lines 1-3). 

When the recursive depth $dep$ has not reached $|Q|$, we will consider each candidate vertex $v$ in $Q[dep].V_{cand}$ (not a duplicate in $M$; line 5). In particular, if candidate vertex $v$ is not connected to some vertex $M[i]$ ($0\leq i < dep$) in the current vertex list $M$ and any vertex in $Q[i].V_{cand}$ ($dep+1\leq i <|Q|$), then it implies that the resulting subgraph $g$ will not be connected and we can terminate the recursive call; otherwise, we can set the matching vertex $M[dep]$ to $v$, and recursively invoke function {\bf Refinement}$_f (G,q,Q,S,M,dep+1,\sigma)$ for the next depth $(dep+1)$ (lines 6-8). Finally, we return the S$^3$AND query answer set $S$ (line 9).


%We show the retrieval and refinement processing in Algorithm~\ref{alg:css}. We adopt a recursive approach to illustrate this process (lines 1-8). If the recursion depth reaches the size of $Q$, we then examine whether the subgraph $g$ formed by the currently obtained vertex list $M$ satisfies the connectivity and AND constraints. Specifically, if the subgraph $g$ is connected and AND($q,g$) is less than $\sigma$, the $M$ is added to $S$ as a subgraph that satisfies both the connectivity and AND constraints for $q$ (lines 1-3). Otherwise, for each candidate vertex $v$ in $Q[dep].V_{cand}$, if $v$ not in $M$ and $v$ is conected to some vertex $M[i]$ (for $0 \leq i \leq dep$) or some candidate vertex in $Q[i].V_{cand}$ (for $dep+1 \leq i \leq |Q|$), we update $M[dep]$ to $v$ and perform the next {\bf Refinement}$_f (G,q,Q,S,M,dep+1,\sigma)$ (lines 4-8). When the recursion concludes, we return the final result $S$ (line 9). Please note that, we can maintain $M$ in the form of a queue or heap to convert recursion into iteration, thereby accelerating retrieval and refinement.

