\section{Conclusions}
\label{sec:conclusions}


In this paper, we formulate a novel problem, \textit{subgraph similarity search under aggregated neighbor difference semantics} (S$^3$AND), which has broad applications (e.g., collaborative team detection and fraud syndicate identification) in real-world scenarios.
To enable efficient online S$^3$AND queries, we propose two pruning strategies (i.e., \textit{keyword set} and \textit{AND lower bound pruning}), to filter out false alarms of candidate vertices/subgraphs. We also devise a tree index on offline pre-computed data, which can help apply our proposed pruning strategies to retrieve candidate subgraphs during the index traversal. Finally, we conduct extensive experiments to confirm the effectiveness and efficiency of our proposed S$^3$AND approach on real and synthetic graphs.

%reduces storage overhead while applying the pruning strategies to minimize the search space. Finally, we completely present an offline pre-computation algorithm and an online S$^3$AND query algorithm to solve the S$^3$AND problem. Extensive experiments on both synthetic and real-world datasets validate the effectiveness and efficiency of the proposed methods.


