\begin{abstract}
    Hypergraphs model complex, non-binary relationships like co-authorships, social group memberships, and recommendations. Like traditional graphs, hypergraphs can grow large, posing challenges for storage, transmission, and query performance. We propose HyperCSA, a novel compression method for hypergraphs that maintains support for standard queries over the succinct representation. HyperCSA achieves compression ratios of $26\%$ to $79\%$ of the original file size on real-world hypergraphs—outperforming existing methods on all large hypergraphs in our experiments. Additionally, HyperCSA scales to larger datasets than existing approaches. Furthermore, for common real-world hypergraphs, HyperCSA evaluates neighbor queries $6$ to $40$ times faster than both standard data structures and other hypergraph compression approaches.
\end{abstract}
%We compare HyperCSA to the approach of Liu et al.~\cite{liu2024} and standard hypergraph data structures, such as the incidence list and incidence matrix.

\keywords{hypergraph, self-indexes, compression, query performance} 

\section[Introduction]{Introduction}

Hypergraphs are powerful tools for modeling complex, non-binary relationships in data. Unlike traditional graphs, where edges connect only two nodes, hypergraphs allow edges to connect any number of nodes, enabling a more accurate representation of real-world systems. This modeling flexibility has made hypergraphs useful in a range of domains, such as social networks and groups~\cite{fang2014,tan2014,yang2012}, recommendation systems for music~\cite{tan2011} or news~\cite{li2013}, authorship of papers, telephone plants~\cite{goldstein1982}, and tagged data from online platforms~\cite{ghoshal2009}. Recently, hypergraph learning algorithms demonstrated strong performance in various applications~\cite{huang2015} including test retrieval~\cite{hu2008}, image retrieval~\cite{liu2011}, protein interactions~\cite{hwang2008}, users' identification via different social networks~\cite{tan2014}, computer vision~\cite{dai2023} and multimedia retrieval~\cite{tan2008}.

In this paper, we introduce a succinct data structure for hypergraphs, HyperCSA. HyperCSA builds on Sadakane's \emph{Compressed Suffix Array} (CSA)~\cite{sadakane2003} and is inspired by RDFCSA~\cite{cerdeira2016}. The CSA is a succinct data structure that can represent a string or a text collection and allows pattern searches like the \emph{Suffix Array} (SA)~\cite{manber90}.

\newpage
Herein, our main contributions are as follows: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item An algorithm, HyperCSA, to compress hypergraphs.%\footnote{The implementation is available at \url{https://github.com/adlerenno/hypercsa}.}
    %\item Information theoretic lower bounds for the class of undirected hypergraphs.
    \item Theoretical bounds on compression size, compression speed, and query performance for HyperCSA.
    \item An thorough experimental analysis of compression ratio, construction time, construction memory usage and query performance of HyperCSA.%\footnote{The test is available at \url{https://github.com/adlerenno/hypercsa-test}.}
\end{itemize}

\section[Related Work]{Related Work} \label{section:related_work}

Like graphs, hypergraphs can be very large. For large hypergraph databases, there is the research branch of partitioning a hypergraph~\cite{schlag2022} and of parallel graph processing with tools like Hygra~\cite{shun2020} and CHGL~\cite{jenkins2018}.

Each hypergraph can be translated into a graph with edges connecting only two nodes, that describes essentially the same data as a hypergraph. Most common methods are the clique expansion, where the rank-k hyperedge is represented by a k-clique, and the star expansion, where a new node is introduced and all nodes of the hyperedge are connected by a new edge with that node. Both methods have multiple downsides regarding compression and querying: They increase the number of objects in the graph by a large factor in comparison to using hyperedges. These expanded graphs are less effective to compress and need many edge lookups to check for an existing hyperedge between multiple nodes. Huang et al.~\cite{huang2015} conclude in the context of distributed hypergraph processing, that expanding hypergraphs to graphs cause major efficiency drawbacks.

Liu et al.~\cite{liu2024} argue likewise for graph processing acceleration. They propose a hypergraph compression method based on the incidence list: They use two one-dimensional arrays, one for the incident hyperedges and one for the nodes and two indexes for each one that contain the start and end of ranges in the arrays. They achieve compression by overlapping the equal nodes (or equal incident hyperedges) of successive hyperedges (or successive nodes). As neither nodes nor hyperedges have an order, they propose a reordering strategy for better compression and less cache misses on access.

\section[Preliminaries]{Preliminaries}

In Appendix~\ref{appendix:abbreviations}, there is a list of symbols used in this paper.

A \textit{hypergraph} is a pair $G = (V_G, E_G)$ with nodes $V_G = \{0, 1, \ldots, p\} \subset \mathbb{N}_0$ and edges $E_G = \{e_0, \dots, e_{m-1}\}$. An edge $e_i = \{v_0, \ldots, v_{n-1}\} \subset V_G$ with $e_i \not= \emptyset$ is an undirected hyperedge of $rank(e_i) = |e_i| = n$. We call all elements in $E_G$ edges regardless of their rank.

HyperCSA can also handle non-simple hypergraphs, meaning $E_G$ can contain the same edge multiple times.\footnote{We require that an edge contains each of its nodes exactly once because having the highest node in an edge twice would break our desired properties of the later introduced $\Psi$.} In \autoref{figure:example_hypergraph}, we visualized the example hypergraph $H = (\{0, 1, 2, 3, 4\}, \{\{0, 1, 2, 3\}, \{1, 2, 3\}, \{2\}, \{0, 1, 2, 4\}, \{2\}\})$.

\begin{figure*}
    \centering
    \caption{The example hypergraph $H = (\{0, 1, 2, 3, 4\}, \{\{0, 1, 2, 3\}, \{1, 2, 3\}, \{2\},$ $ \{0, 1, 2, 4\}, \{2\}\})$ with 5 hyperedges.}\label{figure:example_hypergraph}
    \includegraphics[scale=1]{figures/example_hypergraph/hypergraph.pdf}
\end{figure*}

We define the $degree(v) = |\{e \in E_G : v \in e \}|$ and $M_G = \sum_{e \in E_G} rank(e) = \sum_{v \in V} degree(v)$. For the example hypergraph $H$, $degree(2) = 5$ and $M_H = 13$.

We define a string $T$ of length $|T|$ over an alphabet $\Sigma$ as a sequence $T = T[0] \cdot T[1] \cdots T[|T|-1]$ of characters $T[i] \in \Sigma$ for $i < |T|$. 
We write $T[i, j]$ for a substring $T[i] \cdot T[i+1] \cdots T[j]$ of $T$, $T[i,j] = \epsilon$ if $i > j$, and $T[i..] = T[i, |T|-1]$ for the suffix starting at position $i$. 

We define $rank_T(x, c) = |\{i \leq x : T[i] = c\}|$ as the number of times $c$ occurs in the first $x+1$ characters of $T$ (including the current position). 
Next, we define $select_T(x, c) = \min\{i < |T| : rank_T(i, c) = x\}$ as the position of the $x$-th $c$ in $T$.

The suffix array $SA$~\cite{manber90} of a string $T$ is a permutation of $\{0, \ldots, n-1\}$ such that the $i$-th smallest suffix of $T$ is $T[SA[i]..]$ with $i \in \{0, \ldots, n-1\}$. As permutations are one-to-one mappings, we denote the inverse of the suffix array by $SA^{-1}$. We use the term \textit{T-position} for a position in $T$ and \textit{SA-position} for a position in $SA$.

The compressed suffix array $CSA$~\cite{sadakane2003} of a string $T$ consists of a bitvector $D$ of length $|T|$ and a permutation $\Psi$ of $\{0, \ldots, n-1\}$ (and other data structures that we omit, because we do not need them). 

$D$ is a succinct representation for $T$ using the suffix array $SA$: $D[i] = 1$ marks the first suffix in the sorted suffixes of $T$ that start with a new character, so $D[i] = 1$ if and only if $T[SA[i]] > T[SA[i-1]]$ or $i = 0$. We assume that all symbols of $\Sigma$ have at least one occurrence in $T$. By this assumption, we get $T[SA[i]] = rank_D(i, 1)-1$.

$\Psi[i]$ stores the SA-position $j$ that points to the next T-position $SA[i]+1$ after the T-position $SA[i]$ at SA-position $i$. In one term, $SA[\Psi[i]] = (SA[i] + 1) \mod n$. Using the inverted suffix array $SA^{-1}$, we obtain $\Psi$ as \[\Psi[i] = SA^{-1}[(SA[i] + 1) \mod n]\] 

$\Psi$ is a cyclic permutation, so $\Psi$ contains exactly one cycle. By computing the ranks on $D$ for the SA-positions $i$ in order of the cycle of $\Psi$, we retrieve a rotation of $T$.

\section[HyperCSA]{HyperCSA}

\subsection{Construction}

We begin with outlining the three major steps involved in constructing HyperCSA. First, because we use a CSA as the main component to compress a hypergraph $G$, we encode $G$ as a string $T$. Second, we build the CSA for $T$. Third, we adjust $\Psi$ to ensures that repeated applications of $\Psi$ cycles within the same edge $e \in E_G$. This way, each edge has its own cycle in $\Psi$.

The first step of the construction of HyperCSA is to construct a string $T$ of length $M_G$ over the alphabet $V_G$. We begin by sorting the nodes in ascending order; now, we regard an $e$ as a tuple $(v_0, v_1, \dots, v_{n-1})$ of length $n = rank(e)$. As nodes are only connected once to an edge $e$, we get $v_0 < v_1 < \dots < v_{n-1}$. 

Second, we sort the edges in $E_G$ in descending lexicographic order, so we regard $E_G$ now as an ordered collection. If one edge $e_p$ is a complete prefix of another edge $e_q$, the shorter edge $e_p$ precedes $e_q$ in the order. For two edges $(v_0, \dots, v_{n}) < (w_0, \dots, w_{l})$, we especially have $v_0 \geq w_0$ and thus $v_n \geq w_0$. 

We then copy each node from each edge to $T$. We show the construction of $T$ in \autoref{algo:constructT}. In the example of \autoref{figure:example_hypergraph}, the resulting string is $T = 2 | 2 | 1 2 3 | 0 1 2 4 | 0 1 2 3$. We can reconstruct $E_G$ from $T$ by partitioning $T$ after each position $i$ where $T[i] \geq T[i+1]$. This is because $T[i] < T[i+1]$ if and only if node $T[i+1]$ belongs to the same edge as node $T[i]$, due to the sorting used during construction of $T$.

\begin{algorithm}
    \caption{\(constructT\)}\label{algo:constructT}
    \KwIn{hypergraph $G$}
    \KwOut{string $T$}
    \For{$e \in E_G$}{
        \state{\text{sort $e$ ascending}}
    }
    \state{\text{sort $E_G$ descending}}
    \assign{i}{0}
    \For{$e \in E_G$}
    {
        \For {$v \in e$}{
            \assign{T[i]}{v}
            \assign{i}{i+1}
        }
    }
\end{algorithm}

As the second major step, we build the CSA for $T$. Note, that we use three sorting steps: sorting the nodes, sorting the edges and a suffix sorting of $T$. Arguments on the order of elements can rely on any combination of these sorting steps.

The bitvector $D$ is the unary encoding of $degree(v)$ for the nodes $v \in V_G$. For example, the bit-sequence $1$ represents $degree(v) = 1$. Thus, we construct $D$ by counting the frequency of nodes occurring in $T$ in an array $freq$ of length $|V_G|$. Then, we set $D\left[\sum_{i = 0}^{j-1} freq[i]\right]=1$ for every $j = 0, \dots, |V_G|$ and $D$ to $0$ at all other positions. We build the bitvector $D$ of length $M_G+1$ instead of length $M_G$ to append an additional $1$ at the end. 

For instance, we set $D[0] = 1$ as well as $D\left[\sum_{i = 0}^{|V_G|-1} freq[i]\right] = D[M_G] = 1$, which is the additional appended $1$. By adding the $1$, we can use $select$ to find the interval of $D$ associated to a node $v \in V_G \subset \mathbb{N}_0$: The interval $S(v) = [select_D(v+1, 1), select_D(v+2, 1)) \cap \mathbb{N}_0$ contains the SA-positions $i$ where $T[SA[i]] = v$. We call $select$ with $v+1$ instead of $v$ because $D[0] = 1$ and $rank_D(0, 1) = 1$, but we associate the position $0$ in $D$ with node $0$. Without adding the $1$ at the end of $D$, the expression $select(v+2, 1)$ for $v=|V_G|-1$ becomes undefined.

In the example, we have $D = 1 0 1 0 0 1 0 0 0 0 1 0 1 1$, because two edges are incident to node $0$, three edges to node $1$, five edges to node $2$, two edges to node $3$, and only one edge to node $4$. In \autoref{figure:psi_jumps}, we show $D$ as well as the corresponding node $rank_D(i, 1)-1$ for every SA-position of $D$.

Towards the third step, we need to understand the cyclic permutation $\Psi_{CSA}$. Because we adjust $\Psi_{CSA}$ later, we refer with $\Psi_{CSA}$ to the permutation constructed by the CSA and with $\Psi$ to the permutation that we adjust. Let $i$ be an SA-position $i$ of $\Psi_{CSA}$ with T-position $j = SA[i]$. Proceeding from $T[j]$ to $T[j+1]$, which corresponds to applying $\Psi_{CSA}$ on $i$, we can have the two cases: 

\begin{itemize}
    \item $T[j+1]$ can be a node of the same edge as node $T[j]$, so $T[j+1] > T[j]$. Then, $\Psi_{CSA}[i] > i$, because $i$ and $\Psi_{CSA}[i]$ are the suffix array indices of the suffixes starting at $j$ and $j+1$.
    \item $T[j+1]$ is the first node of a new edge, so $T[j+1] \leq T[j]$. As we sorted the edges in descending order, we get $T[j+1..] < T[j..]$ (see \autoref{theorem:suffix_order} in Appendix~\ref{appendix:proofs}) and thus $\Psi_{CSA}[i] < i$.
\end{itemize}

Note that $\Psi_{CSA}[i] \neq i$ because $\Psi_{CSA}[i] = i$ would form a cycle of length $1$, which contradicts that $\Psi_{CSA}$ has only a single cycle of length $M_G$. We want to adjust $\Psi_{CSA}$ in the way that it contains one cycle for each edge in $E_G$. Since $\Psi_{CSA}$ shows how to reassemble $T$, we only need to adjust the step from T-position $k$ to $k+1$ where the nodes $T[k]$ and $T[k+1]$ belong to different edges. 

Let $i$ be a SA-position with $\Psi_{CSA}[i] < i$ and let $k, k+1, \dots, k+n-2, k+n-1$ be the T-positions of the edge $e$ of rank $n$, where $SA[i] = k+n-1$. For $i$, we need the SA-position $j$ such that $SA[j] = k$. Then, we set $\Psi[i] = j$ and thereby form the cycle for the edge $\{T[k], T[k+1], \dots, T[k+n-1]\}$. For all other SA-position, which are the SA-positions with $\Psi_{CSA}[i] > i$, we have $\Psi[i] = \Psi_{CSA}[i]$.

We could iterate over $\Psi_{CSA}[i]$ by SA-positions $i$ to find the SA-positions where $\Psi_{CSA}[i] < i$. However, we would not know which SA-position $j$ to assign to $\Psi[i]$. Instead, we iterate by T-positions $i$ and follow the cycle of $\Psi_{CSA}$: The last $\Psi_{CSA}[k]$ with $\Psi_{CSA}[k] < k$ points towards the SA-position $\Psi_{CSA}[k] = j$. So, if $i$ is the next SA-position with $\Psi_{CSA}[i] < i$ after $\Psi_{CSA}[k] < k$, we set $\Psi[i]$ to be $j$.

If we iterate by T-positions $i$, we use the sequence of SA-positions $(\Psi_{CSA})^{i}[0]$, because applying $\Psi_{CSA}$ corresponds to increasing the position $i$ in $T$ by one. We do not iterate over $\Psi_{CSA}[i]$, which would be iterating over the SA-position $i$. We decide to start at position $0$ in the suffix array for the following reason: As $\Psi_{CSA}$ is a permutation and forms a cycle of length $M_G$, the SA-position $i$ with $\Psi_{CSA}[i] = 0$ must be greater than $0$, so $\Psi[i] = 0 < i$. This means, $0$ is the SA-position of a first node of an edge. 

Overall, \autoref{algo:adjust_psi} adjusts $\Psi_{CSA}$ to $\Psi$ such that $\Psi$ has one cycle per edge that cycles through the nodes of that edge. We visualized these cycles for our example in \autoref{figure:psi_jumps}.
\begin{algorithm}
    \caption{\(adjust\Psi\)}\label{algo:adjust_psi}
    \KwIn{$\Psi$}
    \assign{current\_position}{\Psi[0]}
    \assign{next}{\Psi[current\_position]}
    \assign{last\_first\_node\_position}{0}
    \While{$current\_position$ is not $0$}{
        \If{$next < current\_position$}{
            \assign{\Psi[current\_position]}{last\_first\_node\_position}
            \assign{last\_first\_node\_position}{next}
        }
        \assign{current\_position}{next}
        \assign{next}{\Psi[next]}
    }
\end{algorithm}

\begin{figure*}
    \centering
    \caption{Example for HyperCSA data structure. It contains the edges $\{0, 1, 2, 3\}$, $\{0, 1, 2, 4\}$, $\{1, 2, 3\}$, $\{2\}$, and $\{2\}$. $rank_D(i, 1)-1$ is the node at index $i$. The black numbers in $\Psi_{CSA}$ are the numbers adjusted by \autoref{algo:adjust_psi}. We visualized the 'jumps' of an edge in $\Psi$ in the same style as the edge in \autoref{figure:example_hypergraph}. The SA-positions with $\Psi[i] \leq i$ are below the numbers, all others are above. The black numbers in $\Psi_\Delta$ are $\Psi[i] - \Psi[i-1]$. We used $t = 5$.}\label{figure:psi_jumps}
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{figures/hypercsa/hypercsa.pdf}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Decompression}

Every SA-position $i < M_G$ belongs to exactly one edge $e$, so given a SA-position $i$, we can extract the corresponding edge $e$ as follows: We calculate every SA-position $j$ with $j = \Psi^k[i]$ for $k = 0, 1, \dots$ until $\Psi^k[i] = i$, which happens the first time for $k = rank(e)$. We then use $rank_1(D, j)$ for every such SA-position $j$ to determine the node. In one expression, $e = \{rank_1(D, \Psi^k[i])-1 : k = 0, 1, \dots\}$.

For the proper decompression, we now need one position per edge. By construction, every edge has exactly one position $i$ with $\Psi[i] < i$. Thereby, it is sufficient to scan $\Psi$ for all positions $\Psi[i] < i$ and decompress each such edge. So overall, we have \[E_G = \{ e = \{rank_1(D, \Psi^k[i])-1 : k = 0, 1, \dots\} : \Psi[i] < i \}.\]


\subsection{Theoretical Analysis of HyperCSA}

We start with analyzing the size of HyperCSA. $D$ has $M_G+1$ bits and we store this as a plain bitvector together with rank and select data structures that support $\bigO(1)$ rank~\cite{vigna2008} and select~\cite{clark1997} queries. For the rank data structure, we need additional $0.25 (M_G+1)$ bits and for the select data structure, we need up to $0.2 (M_G+1)$ bits, so in total, $D$ requires at most $1.45 (M_G+1)$ bits.

Storing $\Psi$ as plain numbers is inefficient, because $\Psi$ has size $M_G$ and would take the same space as $T$. However, $\Psi$ can be encoded significantly smaller than $T$, because $\Psi$ consists of at most $|V_G|$ strictly increasing subsequences. In \autoref{theorem:increasing_subsequence} in Appendix~\ref{appendix:proofs} is the proof of this statement. This property makes $\Psi$ compressible using the differences $\Psi[i] - \Psi[i-1]$.

Overall, $\Psi$ can be stored in space proportional to the zero-order empirical entropy of $T$~\cite{ferragina2010permuterm}. The \textit{zero-order empirical entropy} for $T$ is \[H_0(T) = \sum_{i=0}^{|V_G|-1} \frac{degree(i)}{M_G} \log\left(\frac{M_G}{degree(i)}\right) \text{ with } M_G=\sum_{i=0}^{|V_G|-1} degree(i)\] with $degree(i)$ being the degree of node $i$, which is equal to the frequency of $i$ in $T$, $|T| = M_G$, and $|\Sigma| = |V_G|$. $\Psi$ can be represented in $M_G H_0(T) + \bigO(M_G \log(H_0(T))) \leq M_G \log(|V_G|) + o(M_G \log(\log(|V_G|)))$ bits. 
Using $\delta$-codes and run-length encoding for runs of $1$ in the $\Psi[i+1] - \Psi[i]$, we can reduce the size to $M_G H_k(T)$ with $H_k(T) = \frac{1}{M_G} \sum_{w \in \sigma ^k} |w_T| H_0(w_T)$ with $w_T$ being the concatenation of all symbols following the sequence $w$ in $T$ \cite{cerdeira2016}.

We encode $\Psi$ using the relative values $\Psi[i+1] - \Psi[i]$. For fast practical access, every $t = 128$ position stores the absolute value of $\Psi$. This ensures a constant access time of $\bigO(t)$. In \autoref{figure:psi_jumps}, we show $\Psi_\Delta$ for the example with $t = 5$.

Next, we investigate the construction time of HyperCSA. First, constructing $T$ uses two rounds of sorting integers. First, we do $|E_G|$ sorting steps of edges, which takes $\bigO(|E_G| \cdot R \log(R))$ time. Second, the sorting of the edges takes $\bigO(|E_G| \cdot R \log(|E_G|))$, because a single comparison of two edges can take up to $R$ steps. Constructing $D$ takes $\bigO(M_G)$ for the frequency scan of $T$ and $\bigO(M_G)$ for assigning the unary code to $D$. Constructing $\Psi$ takes $\bigO(M_G)$, as the construction consists mostly of computing the suffix array. \autoref{algo:adjust_psi} traverses the cycle of $\Psi_{CSA}$ once, which takes $\bigO(M_G)$. Thus, the construction time of HyperCSA is $\bigO(|E_G| \cdot R (\log(R) + \log(|E_G|)) + M_G) = \bigO(|E_G| \cdot R (\log(R) + \log(|E_G|)))$.

Hypergraph input files are often sorted, so the nodes in edges appear in ascending order and the edges are also in ascending order. In this case, we can omit the sorting steps and get $\bigO(M_G)$ as construction time. 

\subsection{Queries}

%HyperCSA supports \texttt{degree}, \texttt{exists} and \texttt{contains} queries. 

The \texttt{$degree(v)$} query answers for a node $v\in V_G$, how many edges are incident to $v$. The question can be solved in $\bigO(1)$ by using $D$ only: $degree(v) = select_D(v+2, 1) - select_D(v+1, 1)$, and $select$ queries need $\bigO(1)$~\cite{clark1997}.

The $contains(v_0, \dots, v_{k-1})$ query returns all edges that contain all the nodes $v_0$ to $v_{k-1}$, so $contains(v_0, \dots, v_{k-1}) = \{e \in E_G : \{v_0, \dots, v_{k-1}\} \subset e\}$. 

Answering $contains$ queries is straightforward. As first step, we sort the nodes of the query in ascending order, if they are not already sorted. Second, let $v_{m}$ be the node of the query with the smallest $S(v_i)$ for all $i=0, \dots, k-1$. Every position $i \in S(v_{m})$ belongs to one (different) edge that contains the node $v_{m}$.

We test each edge at a position $i \in S(v_{m})$ individually by traversing $\Psi^k[i]$ if the set $\{\Psi^k[i] : k = 0, 1, \dots \}$ contains the other nodes of the query. We sorted the query because the nodes will appear in the same order in the edge as in the sorted query. However, the edge is allowed to contain more nodes than the query. When the last node of the query $v_{m -1 \mod k}$ is found in an edge, we can output the edge as valid result. Note that we partially decompressed the edge for checking the nodes, so we only need to decompress the rest of the edge.

Due to the construction of $T$, we have multiple conditions to abort and discard an edge:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item When $\Psi^l[i] = i$ for $l\in\mathbb{N}$ before we found all nodes of the query.
    \item If the current node $rank_D(\Psi^l[i], 1)$ is higher than the next node of the query, except if the next node of the query is the lowest node of the query.
    \item If we are at the index $j$ with $\Psi[j] < j$ for the edge and the next node of the query is not the lowest node.
    \item If we look for the lowest node of the query, we are at position $j = \Psi^l[i]$ with $\Psi[j] < j$ and the node $rank_D(\Psi^{l+1}[i], 1)$ is greater than the node of the query.
\end{enumerate}
All these discard conditions origin in the sorting of the nodes of an edge and the cyclic structure of $\Psi$.

The $exists(v_0, \dots, v_n)$ query answers how many edges $\{v_0, \dots, v_n\}$ exist in $E_G$, so $exists(v_0, \dots, v_n) = |\{\{v_0, \dots, v_n\} \in E_G\}|$. In the case of $E_G$ being a multiset, the answer might be larger than one. 

First, we sort $v_0, \dots, v_n$ in ascending order, if they are not already sorted. Second, we set $\mathcal{I}_0 = S(v_0)$. Third, we iterate over the nodes $v_2$ to $v_n$. At the iteration of node $v_i$, we determine $\mathcal{I}_i$ using $\mathcal{I}_{i-1}$, where each $\mathcal{I}_i$ is a subinterval of $S(v_i)$ as follows: We binary search the subinterval $\mathcal{S}$ of $\mathcal{I}_{i-1} \subset S(v_{i-1})$ with the property that for $i \in \mathcal{S}$, $\Psi[i] \in S(v_{i})$. Then, we set $\mathcal{I}_i = \Psi[\mathcal{S}] = [\Psi[\min(\mathcal{S})], \Psi[\max(\mathcal{S})]]$. Fourth, we have $\mathcal{I}_n$ for the highest node $v_n$ now. We check for closed cycles like the previous steps: We search the interval $\mathcal{S}$ of $\mathcal{I}_{n}$ with $\Psi[i] \in S(v_{0})$. Then, we output the length of the interval $[\Psi[\min(\mathcal{S})], \Psi[\max(\mathcal{S})]]$ as the result to the $exists$ query. We prove the correctness of the results of the exists queries in \autoref{theorem:exists_correct} in Appendix~\ref{appendix:proofs}.

\subsection{Runtime Analysis of Queries}

Because we answer $select$ queries on $D$ in $\bigO(1)$, we can get $S(v)$ for $v\in V$ in $\bigO(1)$. The length of $S(v)$ is $degree(v)$. The binary search on $\Psi$ on the interval $S(v)$ thereby needs only $\bigO(\log(degree(v)))$. 

For the $contains$ query, we search the minimum over $k$ values. Then, for every edge of that interval, we perform at most $R$ decompression steps. Each test for the next query node costs only $\bigO(1)$. Thus, we have an average runtime of $\bigO(k + \text{avg}(degree, V_G) \cdot R)$.

For the $exists$ query with $v_0, \dots, v_n$, we have $2(n+1)$ binary searches. Thus, the average runtime of an exists query is $\bigO(n * \log(\text{avg}(degree, V_G)))$. Here, $avg$ is the average of the degrees of $V_G$.

