\section{Proofs} \label{appendix:proofs}

\begin{theorem}\label{theorem:suffix_order}
    Let $T$ be constructed as described above. If $T[j+1] \leq T[j]$, then $T[j+1..] < T[j..]$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
    If $T[j+1] < T[j]$, $T[j+1..] < T[j..]$ is obvious.

    If $T[j+1] = T[j]$: $T[j]$ is the highest node of an edge $(T[i], \dots, T[j])$ with $i \leq j$. Due to the order of nodes, we have $T[i] < T[j]$ if $i < j$ and due to the order of edges, $T[j+1] \leq T[i]$. Thereby, we get $T[j+1] \leq T[i] < T[j]$ if we assume $i < j$, which contradicts $T[j+1] = T[j]$. Thus, $i = j$ and $(T[j])$ is an edge of rank $1$. $(T[j+1])$ must be a loop at the same node $T[j]$, because the edge $(T[j+1], \dots, T[m])$ is lexicographic smaller than $(T[j])$ but starts with the same node $T[j]$. 

    We now have three cases: 

    First, if $j+2 = M_G$: Then we are at the end of $T$, so we get $T[j+1..] = T[j+1] < T[j] \cdot T[j+1] = T[j..]$. 

    Second, if $T[j+2] = T[j+1]$: This case is recursive, so if we prove $T[j+2..] < T[j+1..]$, we get $T[j+1..] = T[j+1] \cdot T[j+2..] = T[j] \cdot T[j+2..] < T[j] \cdot T[j+1..] = T[j..]$. As $T$ is a finite sequence, the recursion of the proof will end. 

    Third, $T[j+2] \neq T[j+1]$: Since $T[j+2]$ is the first node of an edge and the edges are sorted in descending order, we have $T[j+2] \leq T[j+1]$ and thus get $T[j+2] < T[j+1]$. We then get $T[j+1..] = T[j+1] T[j+2] T[j+3..] = T[j] T[j+2] T[j+3..] < T[j] T[j+1] T[j+2] T[j+3..] = T[j..]$.

    Note that the second case proves the following statement: If we do not have multiple loops at a single node, $T$ will not have a run of nodes.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem} \label{theorem:increasing_subsequence}
    $\Psi$ consists of at most $|V_G|$ strictly increasing subsequences.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
First, we show that $\Psi_{CSA}$ consists of at most $|V_G|$ strictly increasing subsequences.

Let $v$ be a node and $S(v)$ the interval of $v$ on $D$. $\Psi_{CSA}$ points towards the suffixes starting at the T-positions $SA[i]+1$ for $i \in S(v)$ for $\Psi_{CSA}$ before \autoref{algo:adjust_psi}. Because $SA$ is sorted according to suffixes of $T$, these SA-positions $j$ with $SA[j] = SA[i]+1$ are sorted. Thus, the SA-positions $S(v)$ form a contiguous increasing subsequence in $\Psi_{CSA}$ for any $v \in V_G$. Thereby, $\Psi_{CSA}$ can be partitioned into at most $|V_G|$ increasing contiguous subsequences. 

Second, we show that adjusting $\Psi_{CSA}$ to $\Psi$ only divides up to one of these contiguous subsequences into a maximum of three sequences.

Given an SA-position $i$ with $\Psi_{CSA}[i] < i$ and $(T[SA[j]], \dots, T[SA[i]])$ the corresponding edge in $T$. \autoref{algo:adjust_psi} sets $\Psi[i] = j$. From sorting the nodes ascending in an edge, we get $T[SA[j]..] \leq T[SA[i]..]$. Equality of suffixes is possible here because $i = j$ is possible. Thereby, $j \leq i$ from the sort order of the suffix array.% and because $\Psi[i] = j$, we get $\Psi[j] \leq i$.

Let $L = \{i : \Psi_{CSA}[i] < i\}$ be the suffix array positions changed by \autoref{algo:adjust_psi} and $F = \{\Psi_{CSA}[i] : \Psi_{CSA}[i] < i\}$ the values. Let $i \in L$ with $\Psi_{CSA}[i] \neq 0$. The suffixes of $F$ are prefixed by the edges: thus, for $k < l \in F$, $SA[k] > SA[l]$ since the edges are sorted in descending order. \autoref{algo:adjust_psi} replaces the suffix of $T$ starting at $SA[\Psi_{CSA}[i]]$ by the next lower suffix $SA[\Psi_{CSA}[j]]$ from $F$. Due to the order of elements in $F$, $\Psi_{CSA}[j]$ is the next higher value in $F$ than $\Psi_{CSA}[i]$. This means, the order of elements stays the same for all positions in $L$ with the exception that for the SA-position $\alpha \in L$ with $\Psi_{CSA}[\alpha] = 0$. Since $\alpha$ is only a single position, it splits only one contiguous increasing subsequence into two and forms one subsequence of its own. Thus, the number of strictly increasing subsequences increases only by up to two subsequences.

Third, in the implementation of HyperCSA, the suffix array construction algorithm building $\Psi_{CSA}$ adds a highest value at the start of the sequence as well as a lowest value at the end of the sequence. This is not mentioned in the paper. Thereby, these two values form the lowest edge and the special case of $\Psi[\alpha] = 0$ belongs to this edge. In $\Psi$, their cycle contains the positions $0$ and $M_G+1$ due to being the highest and lowest value in the input. Removing this cycle is straightforward. The exception of second step of this proof belongs to SA-position $M_G+1$, because at position $0$ is the highest suffix, and is thus removed. This shows that $\Psi$ consists of at most $|V_G|$ strictly increasing subsequences, as each interval $S(v)$ is ordered.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{theorem:exists_correct}
    The $exists$ query can be solved using binary search.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
    First, the binary searches can be used as each interval $S(v)$ is a contiguous increasing subsequence in $\Psi$ as shown by \autoref{theorem:increasing_subsequence}.
    To show this statement, we show that the interval of the last step contains all edges that match $Q$ and that all edges in that interval are exactly $Q$.

    All edges in $E_G$ that match the exists query $Q = \{v_0, \dots, v_n\}$ with $v_0 < \dots < v_n$ are equal (but not identical). Thus, they are sorted next to each other while sorting the edges in descending order. By construction, their nodes follow each other in $T$. %: All suffixes that belong to these edges now have the same prefixes of length $|Q|$ or have the next smaller edge as part of the prefix of their suffix. After $|Q|*exists(Q)$ characters, the characters belong to the next lower edge in $T$, if such an edge exists. 
    The order of suffixes for two equal edges stays the same when using $\Psi$ to get the next node, because the next nodes are equal. Expanding this argument, if we follow the edge that matches $Q$ with the lowest suffix, which is the rightmost of the equal edges in $T$ (because the next edge in $T$ is smaller by construction), and the edge that matches $Q$ with the highest suffix, which is the leftmost in $T$, we never lose any of the equal edges in the interval between them. This shows that the return value of $exists(Q)$ is at least the number of equal edges $Q$.

    % New Proof?
    Let $a \in S(v_0)$ be the lowest and $b \in S(v_0)$ be the highest SA-position in $S(v_0)$ such that the corresponding edge matches $Q$. For every SA-position $i$ with $a \leq i \leq b$, the suffix $T[SA[i]..]$ must be larger than $T[SA[b]..]$ and smaller than $T[SA[a]..]$ and start with $v_0$. The first $|Q|$ symbols of suffix $T[SA[i]..]$ must be equal to that of both $T[SA[a]..]$ and $T[SA[b]..]$, because the suffixes starting at $SA[a]$ and $SA[b]$ are equal for the first $|Q|$ nodes and the suffix array is sorted according to the suffixes. The $(|Q|+1)$-th symbols must also be smaller than the $|Q|$-th symbol of $T[SA[i]..]$ using the same argument. Thus, the edge $e$ containing SA-position $i$ has the suffix $Q$. $e$ cannot contain lower nodes than $v_0$, because $e$ would be lexicographically lower than $Q$ and the suffix $T[SA[i]..]$ would thus be smaller than $T[SA[b]..]$ by the construction of $T$. Thus, all elements within the interval are valid results, so the return value of $exists(Q)$ is lower or equal to the number of equal edges $Q$.

\end{proof}

