\solved{

After pruning harmful tokens in vulnerable layers 
% $[n, n + \Delta_n]$
, the subsequent layers
% (e.g., layers $[10, 31]$ for LLaVA1.5-7B and MiniGPT-4-7B, layers $[7, 11]$ for DeepSeek-VL2) 
operate on incomplete visual representations, lacking fine-grained contextual interactions.
% This impairs cross-modal integration and language refinement, reducing visual understanding.
% Fortunately, these “safety layers” (per Finding-1) are less vulnerable to attacks, making them suitable for reintroducing pruned content. 
To restore utility without compromising robustness, we introduce the Benign Feature Restoration (BFR) module, which selectively reintegrates benign features while avoiding harmful semantics.
Formally, the restored hidden states are computed as: $SH_{img}^{n+\Delta_n} = BFR(\hat{H}_{img}^{n + \Delta_n - 1}, H_{img}^{n + \Delta_n - 1})$.
    % \begin{equation}
    % % \label{eqn:Benign_Features_Restoration}
    %     \label{eqn:img_restore}
    % 	SH_{img}^{n+n_l} = BFR(\hat{H}_{img}^{n + n_l - 1}, H_{img}^{n + n_l - 1})
